WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.120
The text of John Wick is obsessed with rules&nbsp;
and consequences. But there are also a set of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:12.080
creative guidelines and standards behind the&nbsp;
scenes that have helped set the series apart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:18.000
from other action films. These rules explain why&nbsp;
audiences are so captivated by what is essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.640
a simple urban fairytale.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:26.160
In the world of John Wick, those who serve under&nbsp;
the High Table, from assassins to arms dealers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.680
bartenders, provide goods and services in&nbsp;
exchange for specially minted gold coins.&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:38.000
Over the course of the series, John Wick trades&nbsp;
coins for weapons, medical care, lodging, drinks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:44.160
and favors. But trying to determine the monetary&nbsp;
value of a High Table coin is futile. Coins are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:50.480
not about wealth, they're about respect. In the&nbsp;
words of Berrada, who oversees minting the coins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:55.520
and was introduced in Chapter 3...
"This coin of course does not represent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.400
monetary value. It represents&nbsp;
the commerce of relationships."&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:05.680
Astute viewers will note that when offered, no&nbsp;
one in the series has yet turned down a coin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.800
or refused to perform a favor for another&nbsp;
member of their society in exchange for one.&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.760
In a behind-the-scenes featurette, John&nbsp;
Wick producer and co-director David Leitch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.960
refers to coins as tokens of membership.&nbsp;
Each of the first three films introduces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.960
a talisman of sorts that defines&nbsp;
how that film approaches the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.480
Under the Table. In the first film, the&nbsp;
gold coins represent access, the audience's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.600
invitation to the secret underworld.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:40.160
In John Wick: Chapter 2, a second talisman is&nbsp;
introduced: The marker. Markers are golden tokens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:46.240
which flip open like a pocket watch to reveal&nbsp;
a bisected silver plate inside. These plates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:51.920
serve as a record of debt. When one person Under&nbsp;
the Table asks a significant favor of another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:57.200
the one granting the favor has the right&nbsp;
to demand anything in return, at any time.&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.960
The obliged party leaves a bloody thumbprint on&nbsp;
one side of the silver plate. The one granting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:08.480
the favor keeps the marker, and presents it to the&nbsp;
other party when the time has come to call in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:14.640
debt. Only once the favor is complete does the&nbsp;
bearer leave their own bloody thumbprint inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:20.880
the marker. The High Table also keeps a record&nbsp;
of markers to ensure the legitimacy of all debts.&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.240
Someone holding your marker is a serious,&nbsp;
unbreakable commitment. Even among a society of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:32.400
assassins, you are not permitted to kill someone&nbsp;
who holds your marker, nor may you refuse to honor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:39.680
it. In Chapter Two, John initially refuses Santino&nbsp;
D'Antonio's marker, and Santino burns down John's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:46.160
house in response, an action that holds up as&nbsp;
entirely justified Under the Table. In Chapter 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:53.040
Sofia Al-Azwar honors John's marker even after&nbsp;
John is declared excommunicado by the High Table.&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:59.440
Markers are the talisman of obligation. His marker&nbsp;
with D’Antonio tethers John to the world of blood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.240
and guns whether he likes it or not.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:08.480
When the first John Wick begins, John is five&nbsp;
years retired from his life as the Baba Yaga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:14.960
an assassin so legendary that he is feared even&nbsp;
by those he serves. But he's only days separated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:20.800
from the death of his wife, Helen, the person&nbsp;
for whom he left the business. His leave from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:26.960
the service of Russian mob boss Viggo Tarasof&nbsp;
was not easily won. John earned his retirement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:32.960
after the completion of an often invoked but&nbsp;
vaguely described "impossible" task. It is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:38.720
implied throughout the series that escaping the&nbsp;
assassin's life alive is essentially unheard of.&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:44.240
Fate steps in to draw John back into the&nbsp;
underworld after Helen dies an untimely but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:50.480
natural death. Viggo credits Helen's death to&nbsp;
divine retribution for John's life as an assassin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:56.000
John, who fears damnation himself, may&nbsp;
also believe that God is denying him rest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.920
though he pursues it nevertheless.
It’s appropriate then that the third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:06.320
and final talisman, introduced in Chapter 3, is&nbsp;
John's "ticket." This important relic is a Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:12.880
Orthodox cross and rosary that he returns to the&nbsp;
Director, the woman who presumably raised him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:18.960
after his excommunication. The ticket grants him a&nbsp;
swift and secret exit out of New York, where he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:26.240
beset on all sides by assassins looking to turn&nbsp;
in his head for $15 million. Chapter 3's talisman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:31.760
represents escape, and it's no coincidence that&nbsp;
this totem is the only one of the three that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:38.021
broken upon use. Because, while he leaves New York&nbsp;
behind for a time, ultimately, there is no escape.&nbsp;

00:04:39.856 --> 00:04:41.760
It's the first rule the audience learns in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:47.520
John Wick, and it's the one that gets John in the&nbsp;
most trouble. The Continental is a chain of hotels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:53.440
operated by the High Table, with locations around&nbsp;
the world catering to globetrotting assassins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.880
The Continental offers a variety of specialized&nbsp;
services for their clients, such as doctors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:04.480
arms dealers, and tailors who will make you the&nbsp;
perfect kevlar-lined suit. But the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:08.720
thing it provides is peace of mind.
Anyone who conducts the business of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:14.800
assassination on Continental grounds will be&nbsp;
summarily excommunicated by the High Table.&nbsp;

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:22.320
"You know the rules. No business can be conducted&nbsp;
on these premises lest incurring heavy penalties."&nbsp;

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:29.440
Completely cut off from the society's resources,&nbsp;
excommunicated assassins are easy prey for anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:35.440
who wants them dead. Guests are required to leave&nbsp;
all grudges and contracts at the door. That way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:40.560
they can enjoy a drink and a good night's sleep&nbsp;
without worrying about whether they'll wake up.&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:45.840
John breaks this cardinal rule when he kills&nbsp;
Santino D'Antonio in the New York Continental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:52.000
bar at the end of Chapter 2. This leads to not&nbsp;
only his own excommunication, but consequences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:57.520
for anyone who aided the assassination or&nbsp;
his escape from the High Table's judgement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:04.160
Winston nearly loses his position and his life for&nbsp;
offering John a one-hour head start from pursuit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:10.320
The Continental is sacred, not just as a base, but&nbsp;
as a symbol that allows these cold-blooded killers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:12.960
to see themselves as civilized.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:19.120
When one considers the violence and cruelty of the&nbsp;
real world, action movies can become a very guilty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:24.880
pleasure. Gun violence kills about 100 people in&nbsp;
the United States each day, and it's impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:30.720
to ignore how popular culture normalizes or&nbsp;
even romanticizes killing people with guns.&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:36.560
The John Wick series in particular depicts gun&nbsp;
violence as, well, extremely cool. Throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:41.920
the films' nearly non-stop action sequences,&nbsp;
it's next to impossible not to delight in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:47.360
creativity with which John Wick dispatches&nbsp;
his foes. Some tallies put John’s kill count&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.880
at nearly 300 over the course of the first three&nbsp;
films. Not all of them are with guns of course.&nbsp;

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:57.600
But there's a particular, subtle creative&nbsp;
choice that helps keep the John Wick audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:02.880
from stopping to consider the carnage for even&nbsp;
a moment. Despite the outrageous death toll,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:08.800
the series so far features absolutely zero&nbsp;
collateral damage. Apart from some mobsters in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:15.040
over their heads in the first film, who presumably&nbsp;
serve Under the Table, everyone John kills is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:21.040
a killer. This effectively makes homicide as&nbsp;
"in bounds" for John as punching is for a boxer.&nbsp;

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:25.280
When you consider the psychic damage of&nbsp;
decades of films in which thousands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:31.040
random people die. These poor souls trapped in&nbsp;
crumbling skyscrapers destroyed by monsters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:36.320
aliens, and natural disasters. John Wick's&nbsp;
polite isolation of violence is almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:38.720
refreshing by comparison.

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:42.720
Part of the reason why the John Wick&nbsp;
franchise can isolate the scope of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:48.640
violence specifically to the world of assassins&nbsp;
is that, as far as the films are concerned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:53.360
there is nothing but the world of assassins.
In behind the scenes featurettes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:58.000
producer/directors Chad Stahelski&nbsp;
and David Leitch speak of the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:03.120
of the first film as being "heightened like a&nbsp;
graphic novel." But as the series has evolved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:08.640
it's much more apt to say that the world of John&nbsp;
Wick is like a video game. John’s is a world that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:14.640
may appear to be open and fleshed out, but in&nbsp;
fact centers around only one idea or objective.&nbsp;

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:19.840
In the John Wick films, everything the audience&nbsp;
encounters revolves around professional killing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:23.520
The Continental is like a home&nbsp;
base where assassins can return,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:28.720
recover, and upgrade themselves with new&nbsp;
weapons, health, and armor between stages.&nbsp;

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:35.040
The society has its own economy and its own rules&nbsp;
that "players" honor even when no authority figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:40.080
is watching. And since the High Table seems&nbsp;
to freely operate with no threat of police or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:45.360
government interference, there is no outside&nbsp;
authority to enforce the kind of consequences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.320
one might face for their actions in the real&nbsp;
world. Only the rules of the game matter.&nbsp;

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:53.280
The John Wick series adopts the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:59.120
trappings of a virtual world, but when it comes to&nbsp;
the actual filmmaking, there's very little virtual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:04.320
about them. One of the traits that separates the&nbsp;
John Wick films from other hit action franchises&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:10.400
of its time is its relatively spare use of&nbsp;
visual effects in favor of practical stunts.&nbsp;

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:16.880
Chad Stahelski and David Leitch, who co-directed&nbsp;
the first chapter, are the co-founders of 87eleven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:21.680
Action Design, a collective of stunt&nbsp;
performers and coordinators who have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:27.600
working on Hollywood productions for decades.&nbsp;
87eleven’s influence has recently exploded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:34.000
thanks in large part to the success of John Wick.
87eleven employs a wealth of stunt and martial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:40.960
arts expertise and an intricate pre-visualization&nbsp;
process to experiment, design, and rehearse long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:45.600
complicated fight scenes. They hold to&nbsp;
an obsessive standard of quality while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:51.280
also keeping the on-set production times, and&nbsp;
therefore the expense of shooting, under control.&nbsp;

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:56.240
While blockbuster action franchises boast&nbsp;
computer-driven effects shots numbering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:01.840
in the thousands, the John Wick series is about&nbsp;
showing off the elegance of practical stunt work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:08.000
with CGI only taking prominence under specific&nbsp;
circumstances, such as the motorcycle chase in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:13.360
Chapter 3. That's not to say that there isn't&nbsp;
a great deal of CGI at play in John Wick.&nbsp;

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:18.720
Like most modern movies, there are plenty of&nbsp;
computer-generated gun flares, blood splatters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:24.560
hidden cuts and erased wires, but the effects&nbsp;
are supporting the players, not the star.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:30.000
It's a different variety of spectacle more akin&nbsp;
to martial arts films than other modern American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.920
action spectacles.

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:37.680
The John Wick series' signature of long, close-up&nbsp;
action takes is only possible because Keanu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:43.200
Reeves is a performer who is willing to dedicate&nbsp;
countless hours to mastering any skill that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:49.280
script demands. In preparation for each John Wick&nbsp;
film, Reeves put in months of full-time training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:54.720
in martial arts, tactical shooting, and precision&nbsp;
driving. As a result, the filmmakers have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.720 --> 00:11:00.480
rarely had to stage scenes around hiding a stunt&nbsp;
actor. This has also allowed them to maintain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:06.160
Reeve’s connection with the audience throughout.
Director Chad Stahelski was Reeves' stunt double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:11.680
for the Matrix films, and the two have a very&nbsp;
close relationship. This means Stahelski is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:16.720
intimately familiar with Reeves' potential&nbsp;
and limitations, and he playfully exploits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:20.560
Reeves' habit of gleefully throwing&nbsp;
himself into training for a film.&nbsp;

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:26.560
For Chapter 2, Stahelski brought in Brazilian&nbsp;
masters the Machado brothers to help Keanu perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:32.480
his jiu-jitsu, and sent him to firearms training&nbsp;
with world champion sport shooter Taran Butler.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:38.480
The results are dazzling and a little terrifying.&nbsp;
It would be a bad idea to pick a fight with Keanu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:44.240
Reeves. When Reeves suggested that John Wick&nbsp;
ride a horse in Chapter 3, Stahelski assigned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:49.600
him to learn trick riding because just learning to&nbsp;
ride a horse would be "too easy." Did we mention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:54.800
that Reeves also plays creative roles behind&nbsp;
the camera, pitching story and stunt ideas?&nbsp;

00:11:54.800 --> 00:12:00.880
Still, let's remember to give some love to Reeves'&nbsp;
stunt double Jackson Spidell, who gets tagged in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:04.240
to take John Wick's nastiest falls.

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:09.680
Keanu Reeves isn't the only John Wick star&nbsp;
who undergoes intensive stunt training.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:15.440
In fact, it's expected of just about everyone.&nbsp;
Any actor portraying a character who gets actively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:20.800
involved in action sequences is expected to meet&nbsp;
the rigorous standards of the full-time stunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:26.400
performers who make up the armies of nondescript&nbsp;
goons dispatched by John Wick and company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:32.320
The goal is to use doubles as rarely as possible,&nbsp;
allowing the camera to move freely and maintain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:36.560
a connection to the characters throughout&nbsp;
fight sequences. The cast and crew refer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:41.760
to this practice as "The John Wick Program."
Each actor gets trained in a fighting style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:46.800
specific to their character, often built&nbsp;
off of the performer's preexisting skills.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:52.240
Adrianne Palicki, who played Ms. Perkins in the&nbsp;
first film, paired with stunt performer Heidi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:58.240
Moneymaker. Moneymaker, Scarlet Johannsen's Black&nbsp;
Widow double, helped to develop a judo-based style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:03.840
that would allow her to convincingly take down&nbsp;
the larger Reeves. Meanwhile Michael Nyqvist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:10.080
portraying Viggo, was assigned a "drunken master"&nbsp;
style to portray a tough brawler past his prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:14.560
who would have to cheat to win. Common,&nbsp;
playing Cassian in the second film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:21.200
threw himself into gun-fu and knife training. Ruby&nbsp;
Rose played the silent but very lethal Ares. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:26.000
perfected a style based on her boxing skills.
The grand champion of "The John Wick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:31.920
Program, " apart from Reeves himself, is&nbsp;
undoubtedly Halle Berry, who played Sofia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:37.360
Berry aggressively pursued her role in Chapter&nbsp;
3, and then committed to seven months of not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:42.400
just fighting and shooting lessons, but also&nbsp;
training with her character's stunt dogs.&nbsp;

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:45.120
"No. You cannot keep my dog."

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:51.120
In the world of John Wick, the last thing you ever&nbsp;
want to do is piss off the Baba Yaga. Not since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:57.920
Kill Bill's Beatrix Kiddo has American cinema seen&nbsp;
such an unstoppable force of vengeance to roar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:05.280
rampage, and get bloody satisfaction. John Wick&nbsp;
is a killing machine of unprecedented versatility.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:11.440
He’s a living weapon in whose hands anything&nbsp;
from a car, to a pencil, to a library book is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:17.120
a deadly weapon. John Wick can construct a&nbsp;
functioning pistol out of antique gun parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:22.400
in the time it takes you to break down a&nbsp;
door. He is cartoonishly good at violence.&nbsp;

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:26.960
It's difficult to imagine how much further&nbsp;
the John Wick series can take the story and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.960 --> 00:14:32.720
the character in the promised Chapters Four and&nbsp;
Five, which are expected to complete the series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:38.320
The creators have established a set of rules that&nbsp;
have contributed greatly to the films' success.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:43.840
But as the series draws toward its climax&nbsp;
and audience expectations continue to rise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:49.840
Chad Stahelski and company may find adhering&nbsp;
to those rules to be a difficult challenge.&nbsp;

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:55.200
How strict should a film series' style guide&nbsp;
be? Can John Wick expand its scale while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.200 --> 00:15:00.320
holding its shape across two more chapters?&nbsp;
In order to keep the premise from going stale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.320 --> 00:15:05.440
will the creators themselves have to do the&nbsp;
unthinkable, and change the rules of John Wick?&nbsp;

